1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,378
Last night I told you about the

2
00:00:02,378 --> 00:00:03,146
flour tin.

3
00:00:03,326 --> 00:00:05,749
About the letter my mother burned in

4
00:00:05,749 --> 00:00:07,835
the kitchen stove at two in the

5
00:00:07,835 --> 00:00:09,921
morning, on Thanksgiving night,

6
00:00:09,921 --> 00:00:11,267
nineteen forty-four.

7
00:00:11,447 --> 00:00:13,434
And about the one letter at the

8
00:00:13,434 --> 00:00:14,651
bottom of the page.

9
00:00:14,831 --> 00:00:15,551
F.

10
00:00:15,731 --> 00:00:18,219
Grandma Pearl saw it with her own

11
00:00:18,219 --> 00:00:20,707
eyes, standing in the dark in her

12
00:00:20,707 --> 00:00:21,385
bathrobe.

13
00:00:21,565 --> 00:00:23,272
Jack, I know.

14
00:00:23,452 --> 00:00:24,625
Stop bouncing.

15
00:00:24,805 --> 00:00:27,097
Tonight we find out what she did

16
00:00:27,097 --> 00:00:27,742
about it.

17
00:00:28,092 --> 00:00:29,669
Get under the blanket,

18
00:00:29,669 --> 00:00:30,530
both of you.

19
00:00:30,710 --> 00:00:34,071
Tonight is the winter of nineteen forty-five,

20
00:00:34,071 --> 00:00:35,191
and the spring.

21
00:00:35,371 --> 00:00:37,721
It's the night the war in Europe

22
00:00:37,721 --> 00:00:40,732
ended, and every church bell in Mansfield

23
00:00:40,732 --> 00:00:41,687
rang at once.

24
00:00:41,867 --> 00:00:43,697
And it's the night a boy from

25
00:00:43,697 --> 00:00:45,842
our street came home from England,

26
00:00:45,842 --> 00:00:47,672
with a message for my mother.

27
00:00:47,852 --> 00:00:49,141
From my father.

28
00:00:49,491 --> 00:00:52,254
Pearl didn't say one word that night.

29
00:00:52,434 --> 00:00:54,647
She went back up the stairs,

30
00:00:54,647 --> 00:00:55,832
quiet as a cat,

31
00:00:55,832 --> 00:00:58,045
and lay in her bed until the

32
00:00:58,045 --> 00:00:58,993
sun came up.

33
00:00:59,173 --> 00:01:01,524
And in the morning she made the

34
00:01:01,524 --> 00:01:03,647
oatmeal, the same as always,

35
00:01:03,647 --> 00:01:05,618
and she said good morning,

36
00:01:05,618 --> 00:01:07,741
Dorothy, the same as always.

37
00:01:07,921 --> 00:01:10,882
My mother never knew she'd been seen.

38
00:01:11,062 --> 00:01:12,581
Not for a long,

39
00:01:12,581 --> 00:01:13,593
long time.

40
00:01:13,943 --> 00:01:15,238
But that afternoon,

41
00:01:15,238 --> 00:01:17,692
while my mother pushed me around the

42
00:01:17,692 --> 00:01:19,192
block in the carriage,

43
00:01:19,192 --> 00:01:21,373
Pearl climbed up into the attic.

44
00:01:21,553 --> 00:01:24,134
Up there was a cardboard box with

45
00:01:24,134 --> 00:01:26,325
her two boys' school things.

46
00:01:26,505 --> 00:01:28,235
Walter's and Frank's.

47
00:01:28,415 --> 00:01:29,773
Report cards.

48
00:01:29,953 --> 00:01:31,613
Spelling tests.

49
00:01:31,793 --> 00:01:34,872
A Mother's Day card from nineteen twenty-six,

50
00:01:34,872 --> 00:01:36,309
with a crooked heart.

51
00:01:36,489 --> 00:01:38,695
Pearl had kept every one of them.

52
00:01:39,046 --> 00:01:41,613
She took out one of Frank's spelling

53
00:01:41,613 --> 00:01:42,041
tests.

54
00:01:42,221 --> 00:01:43,512
Forty years later,

55
00:01:43,512 --> 00:01:45,089
when she told me this,

56
00:01:45,089 --> 00:01:47,097
she could still describe it.

57
00:01:47,277 --> 00:01:48,820
Big slanting letters,

58
00:01:48,820 --> 00:01:51,539
in pencil, pressing so hard they went

59
00:01:51,539 --> 00:01:52,861
through the paper.

60
00:01:53,041 --> 00:01:55,259
Every F looped at the top like

61
00:01:55,259 --> 00:01:56,072
a fishhook.

62
00:01:56,252 --> 00:01:58,035
She sat on an old trunk in

63
00:01:58,035 --> 00:02:00,711
that freezing attic and held that paper

64
00:02:00,711 --> 00:02:01,534
for an hour.

65
00:02:01,714 --> 00:02:03,744
Then she put it back in the

66
00:02:03,744 --> 00:02:06,149
box and never took it out again.

67
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:08,864
That was my first Christmas,

68
00:02:08,864 --> 00:02:09,878
sweethearts.

69
00:02:10,058 --> 00:02:11,872
I was four months old,

70
00:02:11,872 --> 00:02:13,768
so I don't remember it,

71
00:02:13,768 --> 00:02:14,923
but Pearl did.

72
00:02:15,103 --> 00:02:17,555
A little tree in the front window

73
00:02:17,555 --> 00:02:19,711
with the old glass ornaments,

74
00:02:19,711 --> 00:02:22,386
and tinsel that you saved every year

75
00:02:22,386 --> 00:02:23,650
and hung back up,

76
00:02:23,650 --> 00:02:24,913
strand by strand.

77
00:02:25,093 --> 00:02:27,189
And on the top of the tree,

78
00:02:27,189 --> 00:02:28,197
a paper star,

79
00:02:28,197 --> 00:02:30,681
because the metal for a real one

80
00:02:30,681 --> 00:02:32,233
had gone to the war.

81
00:02:32,584 --> 00:02:35,510
And then, nine days before Christmas,

82
00:02:35,510 --> 00:02:38,516
the news on the radio turned terrible.

83
00:02:38,696 --> 00:02:41,215
The Germans attacked our boys in a

84
00:02:41,215 --> 00:02:44,030
forest in Belgium called the Ardennes,

85
00:02:44,030 --> 00:02:44,919
in the snow.

86
00:02:45,099 --> 00:02:46,783
The Battle of the Bulge,

87
00:02:46,783 --> 00:02:48,257
they called it later.

88
00:02:48,437 --> 00:02:51,258
Tens of thousands of Americans were killed

89
00:02:51,258 --> 00:02:52,802
or wounded or captured.

90
00:02:52,982 --> 00:02:55,584
Every family on Maple Street with a

91
00:02:55,584 --> 00:02:57,814
son in Europe sat by the radio

92
00:02:57,814 --> 00:02:59,078
and didn't sleep.

93
00:02:59,428 --> 00:03:01,357
And right after Christmas,

94
00:03:01,357 --> 00:03:04,252
the government put meat and butter back

95
00:03:04,252 --> 00:03:05,291
on the ration.

96
00:03:05,471 --> 00:03:07,502
More stamps, more points,

97
00:03:07,502 --> 00:03:09,940
more standing in line at the A

98
00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:10,428
and P.

99
00:03:10,608 --> 00:03:13,094
Pearl said it was the hardest winter

100
00:03:13,094 --> 00:03:14,544
since the Depression.

101
00:03:14,724 --> 00:03:16,198
The coal ran short.

102
00:03:16,378 --> 00:03:18,573
The milk froze on the porch.

103
00:03:18,753 --> 00:03:21,222
And Frank came every single evening,

104
00:03:21,222 --> 00:03:22,526
with his arms full.

105
00:03:22,876 --> 00:03:25,863
My father's letters came every week that

106
00:03:25,863 --> 00:03:27,954
winter, on thin blue V-mail,

107
00:03:27,954 --> 00:03:30,120
from the hospital in England.

108
00:03:30,300 --> 00:03:32,021
Short ones at first,

109
00:03:32,021 --> 00:03:34,085
because his hands shook.

110
00:03:34,265 --> 00:03:36,667
The nurse wrote the first few for

111
00:03:36,667 --> 00:03:36,959
him.

112
00:03:37,139 --> 00:03:39,823
Then his own handwriting came back,

113
00:03:39,823 --> 00:03:40,974
small and neat,

114
00:03:40,974 --> 00:03:43,582
every letter straight up and down.

115
00:03:43,762 --> 00:03:45,515
Nothing like a fishhook.

116
00:03:45,866 --> 00:03:48,187
He wrote about the hospital food,

117
00:03:48,187 --> 00:03:50,719
and an English girl who brought tea,

118
00:03:50,719 --> 00:03:52,618
and a Texas boy in the next

119
00:03:52,618 --> 00:03:54,236
bed who sang all night.

120
00:03:54,416 --> 00:03:56,800
And at the end of every letter,

121
00:03:56,800 --> 00:03:57,876
the same line.

122
00:03:58,056 --> 00:04:00,328
Send me a picture of her,

123
00:04:00,328 --> 00:04:00,692
Dot.

124
00:04:00,872 --> 00:04:01,603
Please.

125
00:04:01,783 --> 00:04:04,203
So in February my mother took me

126
00:04:04,203 --> 00:04:07,153
to the photographer downtown and had my

127
00:04:07,153 --> 00:04:09,876
picture made, in a white dress Pearl

128
00:04:09,876 --> 00:04:11,919
had sewn from a flour sack.

129
00:04:12,269 --> 00:04:14,372
The letter that came back was the

130
00:04:14,372 --> 00:04:15,775
longest he ever wrote.

131
00:04:15,955 --> 00:04:18,019
He said he kept my picture in

132
00:04:18,019 --> 00:04:19,229
his shirt pocket.

133
00:04:19,409 --> 00:04:21,381
He said he showed it to every

134
00:04:21,381 --> 00:04:21,789
nurse.

135
00:04:21,969 --> 00:04:24,177
And then, at the very bottom,

136
00:04:24,177 --> 00:04:25,243
one more line.

137
00:04:25,423 --> 00:04:28,003
You never told me what day she

138
00:04:28,003 --> 00:04:29,208
was born, Dot.

139
00:04:29,388 --> 00:04:31,498
And my mother read that line,

140
00:04:31,498 --> 00:04:33,099
and folded the letter,

141
00:04:33,099 --> 00:04:35,355
and put it in her apron pocket.

142
00:04:35,535 --> 00:04:37,242
And she didn't answer it.

143
00:04:37,422 --> 00:04:38,803
Not in that letter.

144
00:04:38,983 --> 00:04:40,725
Not in the next one.

145
00:04:41,075 --> 00:04:43,841
Pearl said that every night that spring,

146
00:04:43,841 --> 00:04:45,708
after the house was asleep,

147
00:04:45,708 --> 00:04:48,060
she heard my mother at the kitchen

148
00:04:48,060 --> 00:04:49,097
table, writing.

149
00:04:49,277 --> 00:04:51,181
Writing and tearing.

150
00:04:51,361 --> 00:04:52,998
Writing and tearing.

151
00:04:53,178 --> 00:04:55,918
In the morning there'd be little pieces

152
00:04:55,918 --> 00:04:58,166
of blue V-mail paper in the coal

153
00:04:58,166 --> 00:04:58,658
bucket.

154
00:04:58,838 --> 00:05:00,359
Pearl never read them.

155
00:05:00,539 --> 00:05:02,722
But she said a woman doesn't tear

156
00:05:02,722 --> 00:05:04,177
up a letter that says,

157
00:05:04,177 --> 00:05:05,566
the eighth of August.

158
00:05:05,746 --> 00:05:07,944
She tears up a letter that's trying

159
00:05:07,944 --> 00:05:08,823
to explain it.

160
00:05:09,173 --> 00:05:12,121
Now, I've told you about Mrs.

161
00:05:12,301 --> 00:05:13,564
Pruitt next door,

162
00:05:13,564 --> 00:05:16,237
the lady who counted on her fingers.

163
00:05:16,417 --> 00:05:18,310
She had a son named Eddie.

164
00:05:18,490 --> 00:05:19,953
Nineteen years old,

165
00:05:19,953 --> 00:05:21,417
red hair, freckles,

166
00:05:21,417 --> 00:05:23,882
the kind of boy who'd climb your

167
00:05:23,882 --> 00:05:25,885
tree to get your cat down.

168
00:05:26,065 --> 00:05:28,608
He was in the infantry in France,

169
00:05:28,608 --> 00:05:30,303
the same as my father.

170
00:05:30,483 --> 00:05:32,183
In the same division,

171
00:05:32,183 --> 00:05:33,560
as it turned out.

172
00:05:33,910 --> 00:05:36,011
In March, Eddie came home.

173
00:05:36,191 --> 00:05:38,416
He'd been hit in the shoulder by

174
00:05:38,416 --> 00:05:40,294
a German shell in the fall,

175
00:05:40,294 --> 00:05:42,659
and his left arm didn't work right

176
00:05:42,659 --> 00:05:43,215
anymore.

177
00:05:43,395 --> 00:05:45,665
So the Army sent him home early,

178
00:05:45,665 --> 00:05:48,147
with a Purple Heart and a discharge

179
00:05:48,147 --> 00:05:48,573
paper.

180
00:05:48,753 --> 00:05:51,250
The whole street came out when the

181
00:05:51,250 --> 00:05:52,352
taxi pulled up.

182
00:05:52,532 --> 00:05:53,391
Mrs.

183
00:05:53,571 --> 00:05:55,915
Pruitt ran down the steps in her

184
00:05:55,915 --> 00:05:58,331
slippers and held him so long the

185
00:05:58,331 --> 00:06:00,455
taxi driver got out and lit a

186
00:06:00,455 --> 00:06:01,187
cigarette.

187
00:06:01,538 --> 00:06:03,185
And here's the thing,

188
00:06:03,185 --> 00:06:04,127
sweethearts.

189
00:06:04,307 --> 00:06:06,589
Eddie had been in a hospital in

190
00:06:06,589 --> 00:06:09,682
England too, before they shipped him home.

191
00:06:09,862 --> 00:06:11,673
The very same hospital.

192
00:06:11,853 --> 00:06:14,047
In the very same ward as my

193
00:06:14,047 --> 00:06:14,616
father.

194
00:06:14,796 --> 00:06:17,429
For six weeks they had beds three

195
00:06:17,429 --> 00:06:17,908
apart.

196
00:06:18,088 --> 00:06:20,212
But Eddie didn't come over to our

197
00:06:20,212 --> 00:06:21,757
house to tell my mother.

198
00:06:21,937 --> 00:06:23,190
Not that week.

199
00:06:23,370 --> 00:06:24,601
Not that month.

200
00:06:24,781 --> 00:06:27,158
Pearl said he wouldn't look at our

201
00:06:27,158 --> 00:06:28,067
porch at all.

202
00:06:28,417 --> 00:06:30,657
Then came the twelfth of April.

203
00:06:30,837 --> 00:06:31,998
A Thursday.

204
00:06:32,178 --> 00:06:34,534
My mother was hanging out the wash,

205
00:06:34,534 --> 00:06:36,756
and Pearl was in the kitchen with

206
00:06:36,756 --> 00:06:37,631
the radio on,

207
00:06:37,631 --> 00:06:39,852
and a man broke into the program.

208
00:06:40,032 --> 00:06:43,131
He said President Roosevelt had died,

209
00:06:43,131 --> 00:06:45,810
that afternoon, at Warm Springs,

210
00:06:45,810 --> 00:06:46,731
in Georgia.

211
00:06:46,911 --> 00:06:48,630
Pearl dropped a plate.

212
00:06:48,810 --> 00:06:50,984
It broke on the floor in three

213
00:06:50,984 --> 00:06:51,492
pieces.

214
00:06:51,672 --> 00:06:53,927
She kept those three pieces in a

215
00:06:53,927 --> 00:06:55,619
drawer for twenty years.

216
00:06:55,969 --> 00:06:57,507
You have to understand,

217
00:06:57,507 --> 00:07:00,451
Roosevelt had been president since before my

218
00:07:00,451 --> 00:07:02,459
mother was out of high school.

219
00:07:02,639 --> 00:07:03,927
Twelve years.

220
00:07:04,107 --> 00:07:06,220
Through the Depression and the whole war.

221
00:07:06,400 --> 00:07:08,208
To a lot of people he wasn't

222
00:07:08,208 --> 00:07:10,209
a president, he was more like a

223
00:07:10,209 --> 00:07:10,661
father.

224
00:07:10,841 --> 00:07:13,775
People stood on Main Street and cried

225
00:07:13,775 --> 00:07:15,520
in front of strangers.

226
00:07:15,700 --> 00:07:17,383
The stores closed.

227
00:07:17,563 --> 00:07:19,769
The churches opened at night.

228
00:07:20,120 --> 00:07:22,439
And the new president was a man

229
00:07:22,439 --> 00:07:24,160
nobody knew much about.

230
00:07:24,340 --> 00:07:26,543
Harry Truman, from Missouri,

231
00:07:26,543 --> 00:07:29,218
who wore round glasses and had run

232
00:07:29,218 --> 00:07:31,027
a men's clothing store.

233
00:07:31,207 --> 00:07:33,727
Frank said he wouldn't last a year.

234
00:07:33,907 --> 00:07:36,472
My father, in his next letter,

235
00:07:36,472 --> 00:07:39,894
said, Truman's an artillery man from the

236
00:07:39,894 --> 00:07:40,664
last war.

237
00:07:40,844 --> 00:07:42,411
He'll be all right.

238
00:07:42,591 --> 00:07:44,193
My father was right.

239
00:07:44,373 --> 00:07:46,695
My father was usually right.

240
00:07:47,045 --> 00:07:50,218
And then, less than four weeks later,

241
00:07:50,218 --> 00:07:51,247
it was over.

242
00:07:51,427 --> 00:07:53,030
In Europe, anyway.

243
00:07:53,210 --> 00:07:55,360
On Tuesday, the eighth of May,

244
00:07:55,360 --> 00:07:58,873
nineteen forty-five, President Truman came on the

245
00:07:58,873 --> 00:08:01,311
radio in the morning and said that

246
00:08:01,311 --> 00:08:03,032
Germany had surrendered.

247
00:08:03,212 --> 00:08:04,431
V-E Day.

248
00:08:04,611 --> 00:08:06,213
Victory in Europe.

249
00:08:06,393 --> 00:08:08,847
The war with Japan was still going,

250
00:08:08,847 --> 00:08:11,162
he said, and there was still work

251
00:08:11,162 --> 00:08:11,582
to do.

252
00:08:11,762 --> 00:08:14,790
But nobody on Maple Street heard that

253
00:08:14,790 --> 00:08:15,199
part.

254
00:08:15,549 --> 00:08:17,829
By that night the whole town was

255
00:08:17,829 --> 00:08:18,114
out.

256
00:08:18,294 --> 00:08:21,247
The factory whistles at the plants blew

257
00:08:21,247 --> 00:08:21,928
and blew.

258
00:08:22,108 --> 00:08:24,487
Cars drove up and down Main Street

259
00:08:24,487 --> 00:08:27,286
honking, with boys sitting on the hoods.

260
00:08:27,466 --> 00:08:29,661
And then the church bells started.

261
00:08:29,841 --> 00:08:31,959
First the Lutheran church,

262
00:08:31,959 --> 00:08:33,752
then the Catholic one,

263
00:08:33,752 --> 00:08:36,277
then every church in Mansfield,

264
00:08:36,277 --> 00:08:37,907
all ringing at once,

265
00:08:37,907 --> 00:08:38,885
for an hour.

266
00:08:39,065 --> 00:08:41,121
Pearl said you could feel it in

267
00:08:41,121 --> 00:08:41,851
your teeth.

268
00:08:42,202 --> 00:08:44,122
And at the steel mill and the

269
00:08:44,122 --> 00:08:46,903
Westinghouse plant, the men walked off the

270
00:08:46,903 --> 00:08:49,353
line at noon and nobody stopped them.

271
00:08:49,533 --> 00:08:51,939
The bars on Main Street gave out

272
00:08:51,939 --> 00:08:54,119
free beer until they ran dry.

273
00:08:54,299 --> 00:08:57,277
The Mansfield paper printed one word on

274
00:08:57,277 --> 00:08:58,422
the front page,

275
00:08:58,422 --> 00:09:00,789
in letters as big as your hand.

276
00:09:00,969 --> 00:09:03,663
And the Victory Garden on our corner

277
00:09:03,663 --> 00:09:06,356
got trampled flat by people dancing.

278
00:09:06,707 --> 00:09:09,041
On Maple Street they put tables out

279
00:09:09,041 --> 00:09:09,841
in the road,

280
00:09:09,841 --> 00:09:12,175
and every family brought something.

281
00:09:12,355 --> 00:09:14,839
Somebody had a ham they'd been saving.

282
00:09:15,019 --> 00:09:16,053
Mrs.

283
00:09:16,233 --> 00:09:18,255
Pruitt made a cake with the last

284
00:09:18,255 --> 00:09:19,077
of her sugar.

285
00:09:19,257 --> 00:09:21,585
Somebody wound up a Victrola on a

286
00:09:21,585 --> 00:09:23,701
porch and played Glenn Miller,

287
00:09:23,701 --> 00:09:26,099
and people danced in the street in

288
00:09:26,099 --> 00:09:26,734
the dark.

289
00:09:26,914 --> 00:09:29,238
My mother danced with me on her

290
00:09:29,238 --> 00:09:29,538
hip.

291
00:09:29,718 --> 00:09:31,823
Pearl said it was the first time

292
00:09:31,823 --> 00:09:34,455
she'd seen her smile since the telegram.

293
00:09:34,805 --> 00:09:36,098
Frank was there,

294
00:09:36,098 --> 00:09:36,906
of course.

295
00:09:37,086 --> 00:09:39,755
He danced with every lady on the

296
00:09:39,755 --> 00:09:41,173
street, even Mrs.

297
00:09:41,353 --> 00:09:43,593
Pruitt, and he made them all laugh.

298
00:09:43,773 --> 00:09:45,837
And then he came to dance with

299
00:09:45,837 --> 00:09:46,525
my mother.

300
00:09:46,705 --> 00:09:48,332
And she said no,

301
00:09:48,332 --> 00:09:50,060
Frank, I'm tired.

302
00:09:50,240 --> 00:09:52,051
In front of everyone.

303
00:09:52,231 --> 00:09:54,583
And Frank stood there with his hand

304
00:09:54,583 --> 00:09:55,725
out for a second,

305
00:09:55,725 --> 00:09:57,942
and then he laughed his big laugh

306
00:09:57,942 --> 00:09:59,488
and went to get a beer.

307
00:09:59,668 --> 00:10:02,628
Pearl saw his face before he laughed.

308
00:10:02,979 --> 00:10:04,558
Late, near midnight,

309
00:10:04,558 --> 00:10:07,402
Eddie Pruitt came across the street.

310
00:10:07,582 --> 00:10:09,084
He'd had a few beers,

311
00:10:09,084 --> 00:10:11,158
the way all the boys had that

312
00:10:11,158 --> 00:10:11,587
night.

313
00:10:11,767 --> 00:10:14,072
He sat down on our porch step

314
00:10:14,072 --> 00:10:15,503
next to my mother,

315
00:10:15,503 --> 00:10:17,729
with his bad arm in his lap,

316
00:10:17,729 --> 00:10:20,034
and for a long time he didn't

317
00:10:20,034 --> 00:10:21,067
say anything.

318
00:10:21,247 --> 00:10:23,191
He just looked at me,

319
00:10:23,191 --> 00:10:24,951
asleep in her arms.

320
00:10:25,301 --> 00:10:27,014
Then he said,

321
00:10:27,014 --> 00:10:27,541
Mrs.

322
00:10:27,721 --> 00:10:29,486
Miller, I was with Walt.

323
00:10:29,666 --> 00:10:30,676
In England.

324
00:10:30,856 --> 00:10:32,307
Three beds apart.

325
00:10:32,487 --> 00:10:34,403
My mother held her breath.

326
00:10:34,583 --> 00:10:37,143
Eddie said, he talked about you every

327
00:10:37,143 --> 00:10:38,043
night, ma'am.

328
00:10:38,223 --> 00:10:39,662
Every single night.

329
00:10:39,842 --> 00:10:42,239
He had her picture in his shirt

330
00:10:42,239 --> 00:10:42,780
pocket.

331
00:10:42,960 --> 00:10:44,794
He showed it to everybody.

332
00:10:44,974 --> 00:10:46,651
And then in January,

333
00:10:46,651 --> 00:10:47,993
he got a letter.

334
00:10:48,173 --> 00:10:49,287
Not from you.

335
00:10:49,467 --> 00:10:51,758
And after that he didn't show the

336
00:10:51,758 --> 00:10:52,869
picture anymore.

337
00:10:53,219 --> 00:10:54,496
My mother said,

338
00:10:54,496 --> 00:10:56,028
a letter from who,

339
00:10:56,028 --> 00:10:56,539
Eddie?

340
00:10:56,719 --> 00:10:58,094
And Eddie said,

341
00:10:58,094 --> 00:10:59,378
he never said.

342
00:10:59,558 --> 00:11:01,931
But he read it a hundred times,

343
00:11:01,931 --> 00:11:02,391
ma'am.

344
00:11:02,571 --> 00:11:04,618
And he asked the nurse for a

345
00:11:04,618 --> 00:11:05,276
calendar.

346
00:11:05,456 --> 00:11:07,562
And he sat in that bed with

347
00:11:07,562 --> 00:11:10,526
the calendar, counting on his fingers,

348
00:11:10,526 --> 00:11:11,696
back and forth,

349
00:11:11,696 --> 00:11:13,490
the way my mother does.

350
00:11:13,670 --> 00:11:15,915
And Eddie looked over at his own

351
00:11:15,915 --> 00:11:18,300
mother, laughing at a table in the

352
00:11:18,300 --> 00:11:20,195
street, and he looked away.

353
00:11:20,545 --> 00:11:22,019
Then Eddie stood up.

354
00:11:22,199 --> 00:11:24,803
He said, I promised Walt I'd tell

355
00:11:24,803 --> 00:11:27,249
you two things when I got home.

356
00:11:27,429 --> 00:11:29,740
I've been putting it off since March.

357
00:11:29,920 --> 00:11:31,116
I'm sorry.

358
00:11:31,296 --> 00:11:32,705
The first thing is,

359
00:11:32,705 --> 00:11:33,966
he's coming home.

360
00:11:34,146 --> 00:11:36,572
They're sending him home this summer.

361
00:11:36,752 --> 00:11:39,122
And my mother started to cry,

362
00:11:39,122 --> 00:11:40,184
and she said,

363
00:11:40,184 --> 00:11:41,002
thank God.

364
00:11:41,182 --> 00:11:42,308
Thank God.

365
00:11:42,488 --> 00:11:44,355
What's the second thing,

366
00:11:44,355 --> 00:11:44,821
Eddie?

367
00:11:45,172 --> 00:11:47,466
And Eddie Pruitt put on his cap,

368
00:11:47,466 --> 00:11:48,901
and he looked at me,

369
00:11:48,901 --> 00:11:50,837
and he looked at my mother.

370
00:11:51,017 --> 00:11:52,223
And he said,

371
00:11:52,223 --> 00:11:54,435
Walt said to tell you,

372
00:11:54,435 --> 00:11:54,837
Mrs.

373
00:11:55,017 --> 00:11:55,864
Miller.

374
00:11:56,044 --> 00:11:57,530
He can count too.

375
00:11:57,880 --> 00:12:00,144
Tomorrow night, sweethearts,

376
00:12:00,144 --> 00:12:02,812
I'll tell you about the summer of

377
00:12:02,812 --> 00:12:04,429
nineteen forty-five.

378
00:12:04,609 --> 00:12:06,768
The day my father came home on

379
00:12:06,768 --> 00:12:08,424
the train to Mansfield,

380
00:12:08,424 --> 00:12:09,503
in his uniform,

381
00:12:09,503 --> 00:12:10,367
with a cane,

382
00:12:10,367 --> 00:12:12,527
and a little girl he had never

383
00:12:12,527 --> 00:12:14,902
seen waiting on the platform in a

384
00:12:14,902 --> 00:12:15,766
white dress.

385
00:12:15,946 --> 00:12:17,717
And what he did when he saw

386
00:12:17,717 --> 00:12:20,013
the paper on the kitchen table with

387
00:12:20,013 --> 00:12:21,193
my birthday on it.

388
00:12:21,373 --> 00:12:22,383
Goodnight.

389
00:12:22,563 --> 00:12:25,683
Grandma Ruth will be right here tomorrow

390
00:12:25,683 --> 00:12:26,151
night.
